00:54:40.420 --> 00:55:02.560
<v Mads>I definitely felt like there is a thing inside of me that's-- yeah, there's a sadness inside of me that will be fine. I'm good at hiding it. I'm good at not addressing it. But then when I'm alone with someone in this intimate setting, it flares up inside of me, and something flares up inside of them too.
<v Mads>我绝对感觉到我内心有一种东西是——是的，我内心有一种会好起来的悲伤。我很擅长隐藏它。我很擅长不解决它。但当我和某人在这种亲密的环境中单独相处时，它在我内心爆发，也让他们内心的东西爆发了。

00:55:02.560 --> 00:55:24.160
<v Mads>I used to worry that the things that I had been through, my traumas and sadnesses, ruined me for other people, just people who wanted to have fun or were cool and nice. In a way, it's kind of lovely to feel like, oh, I see this in everyone now.
<v Mads>我曾经担心我所经历的事情、我的创伤和悲伤会毁了我，让别人无法接受，只是那些想玩得开心或者很酷很友善的人。在某种程度上，感觉像是，噢，我现在在每个人身上都能看到这一点，这种感觉挺可爱的。

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:50.750
<v Elna Baker>I thought it would be a sign of recovery if people were moving on, cutting loose, leaving the past year behind. But instead, it seemed like they felt very aware of what they've been through, what everyone is feeling. That decision to not look away, to embrace the sadness, that's a sign of recovery too.
<v Elna Baker>我以为如果人们继续前进、打破束缚、把过去的一年抛在脑后，会是康复的迹象。但相反，看起来他们对自己的经历以及每个人的感受感到非常清醒。那种不移开视线、拥抱悲伤的决定，那也是康复的迹象。

